body:
## Summary

Browser Commander should provide a first-class, privacy-aware recording format for a browser session: full DOM checkpoints, live control state, incremental changes, browser events, screenshots, and an offline timeline viewer/replayer.

This is a general observability primitive for browser automation, not only a test-runner feature. It should work for long-lived agents, human-in-the-loop flows, troubleshooting, audit trails, and `browser-commander/tests` failure artifacts.

## Current gap (reviewed against JavaScript 0.17.2 / current `main`)

The building blocks exist, but every consumer has to assemble them itself:

- `commander.content()` can capture the current serialized HTML.
- navigation, network, dialog, trigger, and interaction layers already observe useful lifecycle events.
- `browser-commander/tests` currently writes only an error JSON file and one viewport screenshot on failure (`writeFailureArtifacts`).
- There is no public recorder, checkpoint, DOM diff/mutation stream, versioned trace schema, or replay viewer.

Current source references:

- [`writeFailureArtifacts()` writes error JSON plus one screenshot](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/90a53ff11581d243fbd963593ec0fd7d33efd1b1/js/src/tests/index.js#L501-L551)
- [the public exports include page content and lifecycle helpers but no recorder](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/90a53ff11581d243fbd963593ec0fd7d33efd1b1/js/src/exports.js)
- [the page/session layer already owns navigation-scoped listeners and cleanup](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/90a53ff11581d243fbd963593ec0fd7d33efd1b1/js/src/core/page-session.js)

A downstream automation therefore had to implement all of the following outside Browser Commander:

1. save complete page markup at named moments, including terminal/error states;
2. read live values of inputs, textareas, radios, checkboxes, and custom select controls;
3. diff each reading as `{ before, after, actor }` records;
4. correlate changes with navigation/stage/action timestamps;
5. take stable screenshots at each human confirmation point;
6. retain and sweep sensitive traces independently from normal logs.

`page.content()` alone is not a replay: current `value`, `checked`, `selected`, focus, scroll, canvas, open shadow roots, and runtime-generated state may not be represented by serialized markup. Independent screenshots and log lines also lack a shared ordering and page/frame identity.

## Proposed public API

The exact names are open, but the common path should be small:

```js
const trace = await commander.startTrace({
  output: './artifacts/run.bc-trace',
  mode: 'checkpoints', // 'continuous' | 'retain-on-failure'
  screenshots: 'checkpoints',
  dom: {
    html: true,
    liveControlState: true,
    mutations: true,
    openShadowRoots: true,
  },
  events: [
    'navigation',
    'interaction',
    'console',
    'pageerror',
    'dialog',
    'requestfailed',
    'download',
  ],
  privacy: {
    redactSelectors: ['input[type=password]', '[data-private]'],
    redactAttributes: ['authorization'],
  },
});

await trace.checkpoint('form-filled', { actor: 'automation' });
await trace.checkpoint('human-approved', { actor: 'user' });
await trace.stop();
```

`browser-commander/tests` should be able to opt into the same facility without custom hooks:

```js
browserTest('scenario', fn, {
  trace: 'retain-on-failure',
  screenshots: 'only-on-failure',
});
```

## Portable trace format

Use one engine-neutral, versioned format for JavaScript, Python, and Rust. A zip-compatible bundle would make it inspectable without a library:

```text
manifest.json
events.ndjson
checkpoints/0001.html
checkpoints/0001.state.json
checkpoints/0001.png
mutations/0001.ndjson
artifacts/...
```

Important properties:

- schema version, Browser Commander version, engine/browser/platform metadata;
- monotonically increasing sequence numbers plus wall-clock and monotonic timestamps;
- browser-context, page, navigation, frame, and action IDs on every event;
- a full base snapshot followed by ordered mutation batches, with later full checkpoints for recovery;
- semantic control state separate from HTML serialization;
- explicit records for dropped events, size limits, timeouts, and partial/corrupt termination;
- content-addressed optional resources to avoid duplicating large assets;
- deterministic ordering so traces can be diffed in CI.

The recorder must be best-effort: inability to write one artifact must be recorded but must not stop the automation unless the caller explicitly requests strict mode.

## Offline viewer and replay

A bundled CLI or static viewer should:

- open a trace locally with no server requirement;
- scrub the event timeline and jump between checkpoints;
- show DOM/state changes as before/after diffs and optionally animate mutation batches;
- show correlated screenshots, interactions, console/page errors, failed requests, dialogs, and downloads;
- support a snapshot-only fallback when continuous mutation capture was disabled;
- keep page scripts disabled and network access blocked by default, rendering untrusted captured content in a sandbox.

This is diagnostic replay, not a promise to reproduce server behavior or execute the original application code.

## Privacy and safety requirements

Browser traces routinely contain credentials and personal data. Safe defaults are part of the feature:

- mask password controls and common authorization/cookie headers by default;
- do not record cookies, storage, request/response bodies, file-upload bytes, or cross-origin resources unless explicitly enabled;
- allow selector-, attribute-, URL-, header-, and callback-based redaction before bytes reach disk;
- expose maximum bundle size, per-resource limits, retention helpers, and an `off` mode;
- write local artifacts with owner-only permissions;
- make the viewer inert: no captured scripts, navigation, form submission, or external requests by default;
- document that enabled HTML/form-value capture can still contain sensitive data.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A consumer can start/stop a recorder and create named checkpoints without accessing the raw Playwright/Puppeteer page.
- [ ] A checkpoint contains full HTML, live form/control state, URL/frame metadata, and (when enabled) a screenshot from the same logical moment.
- [ ] Continuous mode preserves ordered DOM mutation batches across SPA updates and full navigations.
- [ ] Navigation, Browser Commander interactions, console/page errors, dialogs, request failures, and download lifecycle events share one ordered timeline.
- [ ] The bundle is schema-versioned, documented, and readable from JavaScript, Python, and Rust (phased implementation is fine, but parity gaps must be explicit in `docs/feature-parity.md`).
- [ ] A local offline viewer can seek, diff, and replay/animate captured DOM changes with scripts and network disabled by default.
- [ ] Redaction happens before persistence and is covered by tests proving secrets are absent from every bundle member.
- [ ] Truncated writes, page closure, navigation during capture, and size limits produce a readable partial trace rather than corrupting the run.
- [ ] `browser-commander/tests` supports `trace: 'retain-on-failure'` and uses this format instead of a separate artifact mechanism.

## Suggested delivery stages

1. Versioned checkpoint bundle and redaction.
2. Unified event timeline and `browser-commander/tests` integration.
3. Mutation recording and offline viewer/replay.
4. Cross-language writers/readers and parity tests.

## Related work

- #53 introduced `browser-commander/tests`, but its current failure evidence is limited to error JSON plus a screenshot.
- #59 exposed `content()`/`innerText()`/`evaluate()`, which are useful low-level building blocks but do not provide coordinated recording.
- #39 documents raw-page access as an escape hatch; this feature should remove the need for each consumer to build a private recorder on that escape hatch.
